rom ep-exdos13.rom page 0 cpu z80
default observed

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The rest of the block-transfer set-up at $29E4. On the way in: HL = the drive's block, IX =
# the file's block (IX+$17-$1A its length, IX+$20 flags, IX+$21 its first cluster, IX+$23 a
# known cluster and IX+$25 that cluster's index in the file); the stack holds the transfer's
# count - 1, then the 32-bit position of its last byte, then that byte's cluster index.

@2A31 XFER_FILE_LAST
  The file's last byte (length - 1, in DE:BC; Z when the file is empty) as a cluster index
  too, then the transfer checked against the file's length:
  1. The alternate HL:(stack) = (length - 1) - (the transfer's last byte); carry: the
     transfer runs past the file's end. AF' keeps that carry and NZ for an empty file.
  2. IY+$48 bit 0 set: a read ($2AD6). Otherwise a write: error $A7 ("Read only file") for
     IX+$20 bit 5; else IX+$20 bit 6 is set (the file changed) and the clusters the write
     needs past the file's last one are added to its chain (ALLOC_CLUSTERS).
  .2A31 Push the file's length - 1
  .2A33 Not empty: DE = its last cluster index ($2AF8)
  .2A36 HL', DE' = the file's length - 1
  .2A3A BC = the transfer's last cluster index
  .2A3B Index too big ($9A)? Drop the three words and return
  .2A3E BC' = the transfer's last byte, low word
  .2A40 The file's last byte - the transfer's, low word
  .2A42 Low word to the stack; HL' = the last byte's high word
  .2A44 High word: carry when the transfer runs past the end
  .2A47 NZ: the file has no cluster (DE = $FFFF)
  .2A49 Keep those flags in AF'
  .2A4A A read?
  .2A51 Read-only file?
  .2A55 Error $A7: Read only file
  .2A59 The file has changed
  .2A5E HL = the transfer's last cluster index
  .2A60 DE = the clusters the file has
  .2A64 BC = the clusters to add
  .2A68 None to add?
  .2A6A Allocate them as a chain: BC = its first cluster
  .2A6D Disk full?
  .2A6F DE = the file's last cluster index
  .2A70 The file had no cluster?
  .2A74 Keep the new chain's first cluster
  .2A75 DE = the file's last cluster ($2B2E)
  .2A7B Its FAT entry links on to the new chain

@2A80 XFER_DROP3
  An error: drops the three words the set-up keeps on the stack and returns A (NZ).
  .2A80 The transfer's last byte, low word

@2A81 XFER_DROP2
  An error: drops two words and returns A (NZ).

@2A84 XFER_NEW_FIRST
  The file had no cluster: the new chain's first cluster becomes the file's first cluster and
  its known one, with index 0 (A = 0 after ALLOC_CLUSTERS).
  .2A84 IX+$21: the file's first cluster
  .2A8A IX+$23: the known cluster
  .2A90 IX+$25: its index, 0

@2A96 XFER_WR_LENGTH
  A write: the file's length becomes the transfer's last byte + 1 when the file was empty or
  the transfer runs past its end.
  .2A96 BC, DE = the transfer's last byte; HL = the high word of the gap
  .2A97 The flags of step 1
  .2A98 The file was empty?
  .2A9A Inside the file: the length stays

@2A9C XFER_LAST_PLUS1
  DE:BC = the transfer's last byte + 1.

@2AA2 XFER_SET_LENGTH
  Stores DE:BC as the file's length (IX+$17-$1A).

@2AAE XFER_WR_PAST
  HL:BC = R - 1, R being the bytes the file had after the transfer's last byte (the gap of
  step 1, negative when the write runs past the old end).
  .2AAE BC = the gap's low word
  .2AAF DE = the count - 1, for the caller
  .2AB2 Count the 32-bit gap down by one

@2AB6 XFER_PAST_COUNT
  IY+$59 = the bytes the write puts past the file's old end, plus 1 (1 - R, at most $FFFF);
  0 when the file has more bytes after the write.
  Exit: A = 0, Z; the register sets swapped back.
  .2AB6 R - 1 negative: the write reaches the old end?
  .2ABA HL = $FFFF?
  .2ABD $FFFF when R - 1 is below -$10000
  .2AC2 HL = 1 - R
  .2AC5 Not 0: that is the count
  .2AC7 1 - R = $10000: $FFFF

@2ACA XFER_PAST_NONE
  The write ends inside the old file: 0.

@2ACD XFER_PAST_SET
  Stores HL in IY+$59 and returns A = 0.
  .2AD3 The main registers back

@2AD6 XFER_RD_CHECK
  A read: returns at once when the transfer ends inside the file; error $E4 (end of file) for
  an empty file.
  .2AD6 The flags of step 1
  .2AD7 DE = the gap's low word
  .2AD8 HL = the count - 1; the drive's block back on the stack
  .2AD9 The file has no cluster: error $E4
  .2ADB Inside the file: done, A = 0
  .2ADE The gap's high word $FFFF (under 64K past the end)?

@2AE2 XFER_RD_CUT
  A read that runs past the end: when part of it is inside the file, the count (IY+$4B)
  becomes the bytes up to the end and IY+$48 bit 7 marks the read cut short; otherwise error
  $E4 (end of file).
  .2AE2 Error $E4: end of file
  .2AE4 Too far past the end?
  .2AE6 HL = the count - 1 + the gap
  .2AE7 Nothing left inside the file?
  .2AE9 HL = the bytes up to the end
  .2AEA The new count
  .2AF0 Cut short: $E4 after the bytes are read

@2AF5 XFER_RD_DONE
  Takes the drive's block back into HL and returns A with flags from it.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Inside $2AF8: a byte position in DE:BC split into cluster index, sector and offset

@2B16 XFER_POS_SHIFT
  Shifts D:E:A (the position's sector number) right by one bit.

@2B1B XFER_POS_NEXT
  The loop shifts the sector number right once per bit of the drive's sectors per cluster
  (the count at HL+11, + 1, in B); then DE = the cluster index, which must be under $8000.
  .2B1B Shift again
  .2B1D D = 0?
  .2B1F No: error $9A
  .2B21 Index $8000 or more?
  .2B25 DE = the cluster index
  .2B27 A = the sector in the cluster
  .2B28 Hides LD A,$9A at $2B29: error $9A, NZ, for a too big index
  .2B2B BC = the byte in the sector
  .2B2C HL = the drive's block back

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Inside $2B2E: the cluster with a given index in the file, from the known one or the first

@2B4B XFER_WALK_FROM
  BC = the steps to go (HL on entry); HL = the drive's block again.

@2B4D XFER_CLUS_WALK
  Follows the chain BC steps from cluster DE. A chain that ends first is error $9A.
  Exit: Z, A = 0 and DE = the cluster.
  Error: A = $9A, NZ.
  .2B4D Steps left?
  .2B4F No: DE = the cluster
  .2B51 DE = the next cluster (FAT_GET_ENTRY)
  .2B54 Not the end of the chain ($FFFF)? Go on
  .2B58 Hides POP HL at $2B59, the way in when no cluster is known
  .2B5A Error $9A
  .2B5C NZ

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Action 3 (help string)

@2B5E EXT_HELP
  Extension action 3 (help string; $0037): HELP alone writes the version line; HELP EXDOS
  writes the version, the copyright and the command list, and claims the action. Nothing is
  done when the byte at IY-$46 is not 0.
  Entry: B = the length of the help word (0 for HELP alone); DE = the help string (counted);
  C = 3; IY = the workspace + $80.
  Exit: C = 0 when HELP EXDOS was answered; otherwise C is kept, so the next ROM answers too.
  Changes: AF, BC, DE, HL, BC', DE', HL'.
  Keeps: IX, IY.
  .2B5E Commands and help off?
  .2B63 A help word given?

@2B66 HELP_EXDOS_VER
  Writes message $37 ("EXDOS version 1.3" and a new line) to channel $FF. The EXX in front of
  MSG_TO_DEFCH's own one leaves the register sets as they were.
  Entry: IY.
  Exit: A = EXOS's status.
  Changes: AF, BC', DE'.
  Keeps: BC, DE, HL, IX, IY.
  .2B67 Message $37: "EXDOS version 1.3\"

@2B69 MSG_TO_DEFCH
  Writes message A (MSG_TO_BUFFER: German or English) to channel $FF, then swaps the register
  sets (EXX): two calls in a row leave them as they were.
  Entry: A = the message number, IY.
  Exit: A = EXOS's status; the register sets swapped.
  Changes: AF, BC, DE, HL, BC', DE', HL'.
  Keeps: IX, IY.
  .2B6C DE = the text, past its count byte
  .2B6D C = its length

@2B6E WRITE_DEFCH
  Writes C bytes from DE to channel $FF (EXOS 8), then swaps the register sets (EXX).
  Entry: C = the length (1-255), DE = the bytes.
  Exit: A = EXOS's status; the register sets swapped.
  Changes: AF, BC, DE, HL, BC', DE', HL'.
  Keeps: IX, IY.
  .2B6F BC = the length
  .2B70 Channel $FF

@3BE2 CMD_NAME_MATCH
  Compares a typed word with a counted name of the command table: the lengths and every
  character (the typed ones in capitals, TO_UPPER) must be the same.
  Entry: HL = the table entry (its count byte); DE = the word's count position (its characters
  from DE+1); B = the word's length.
  Exit: NC, NZ and A = 1 for a match, HL past the name (at the handler's address).
  Error: C and Z, HL past the name, when the name differs (or the word is longer); C and NZ,
  HL kept, when the word is shorter than the name.
  Changes: AF, HL.
  Keeps: BC, DE, IX, IY.
  .3BE4 A = the word's length
  .3BE5 B = the name's length
  .3BE6 Word shorter than the name?
  .3BE9 Compare the length and every character

@2B75 HELP_EXDOS
  HELP with a word: only HELP EXDOS is answered.
  1. Writes the version line, message $38 (spaces), "Enterprise disk system" ($3A), $38
     again, the copyright line at $2BBC (32 bytes) and "Commands avaliable:" ($3B).
  2. Lists the command table from $2C58: every name whose last character has bit 5 clear
     (a letter), each followed by a space, then a new line, and C = 0.
  Each call to $2B69 or $2B6E swaps the register sets; the list runs in the other set.
  .2B75 The table's "EXDOS"
  .2B78 Is the help word EXDOS?
  .2B7B No: leave it to other ROMs
  .2B7F Message $38: spaces
  .2B84 Message $3A: "Enterprise disk system\"
  .2B8E The copyright line, 32 bytes
  .2B96 Message $3B: "\\Commands avaliable:\\"
  .2B9B HL = the command table

@2FD9 CHAR_TO_DEFCH
  Writes character B to channel $FF (EXOS 7).
  Entry: B = the character.
  Exit: AF and BC as on entry.
  Changes: DE.
  Keeps: AF, BC, HL, IX, IY.

@2B9E HELP_CMD_LIST
  Writes the command names whose last character has bit 5 clear: a name and a space each,
  then a new line and C = 0 (the help is answered).
  .2B9E C = the name's length
  .2B9F DE = its characters
  .2BA4 HL = its last character
  .2BA5 A letter (bit 5 clear) is listed
  .2BA9 HL = the next entry; a name with bit 5 set is skipped
  .2BAC Channel $FF
  .2BAF A space after it
  .2BB4 The table's end (0)?
  .2BB8 C = 0: the help action is answered
  .2BB9 A new line

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Action 2 (command string)

@3E14 DRIVE_PREFIX
  Is the command word a drive, a letter and ":"?
  Entry: DE = the word's count position (its characters from DE+1); B = its length.
  Exit: NC: A' = the drive (1 = A), HL = past the colon; Z when the word is just "X:".
  Error: C when the second character is not ":" or the first is not a letter.
  Changes: AF, HL, AF'.
  Keeps: BC, DE, IX, IY.
  .3E16 HL = the second character
  .3E19 ":"?
  .3E1B C, NZ: not a drive
  .3E1E The first character, in capitals
  .3E24 Above "Z"?
  .3E28 Below "A"?
  .3E2B The drive: 1 for A
  .3E2C A' = the drive
  .3E2D Z: the word is two characters long

@2BDC CMD_TRY_DRIVE
  Not a command of the table: a word "X:" (or "X:" followed by a space or a tab) selects the
  default drive through the handler at $2D92, run like a command; anything else is left to
  other ROMs.
  .2BDC A drive?
  .2BDF No: not EXDOS's
  .2BE0 Just "X:"?
  .2BE2 The character after the colon
  .2BE7 Neither space nor tab: not EXDOS's

@2BEA CMD_DRIVE
  Runs the drive handler at $2D92 with the word's length taken as 2.
  .2BEC The handler: drive A' becomes the default drive

@2BF1 EXT_COMMAND
  Extension action 2 (command string; $0033): looks the command word up in the command table
  at $2C58 (counted names in order of length, each followed by its handler's address).
  1. A name whose last character has bit 7 set runs at once (CMD_JUMP); other names run only
     when IY-$2B bit 0 is set (no EXDOS command running) and through CMD_RUN.
  2. A word not in the table may be a drive (CMD_TRY_DRIVE).
  Nothing is done when the byte at IY-$46 is not 0.
  Entry: B = the command word's length; DE = the command string (counted); C = 2; IY = the
  workspace + $80.
  Exit: as the handler leaves it; C kept when the command is not EXDOS's.
  Changes: AF, BC, DE, HL, IX, AF'.
  Keeps: IY.
  .2BF1 Commands and help off?
  .2BF6 HL + 2 = the table's first entry

@2BF9 CMD_SEARCH
  The next table entry. A word shorter than the entry's name ends the search: the names that
  follow are no shorter.
  .2BF9 Shorter word (from CMD_NAME_MATCH)? Try a drive
  .2BFB HL = the next entry
  .2BFD The table's end?
  .2C01 This name?
  .2C04 No
  .2C06 The name's last character
  .2C08 Bit 7 to carry: a name run at once
  .2C0C An EXDOS command running? Then not this one

@2C11 CMD_FOUND
  HL = the handler's address; carry (a bit-7 name) jumps to it at once.

@2C17 CMD_RUN
  Runs a handler with CMD_FINISH ($2D41) as its return address:
  1. Clears IY-$2B bit 0 (an EXDOS command is running).
  2. Moves the text after the command word, its leading spaces and tabs (and 0 bytes)
     dropped, to the front of the string as a counted, zero-ended text.
  3. Empties the output buffer, gives the file block at IY+$FD drive 0 ($3D10), marks the
     channels at IY-$2C and IY-$2D as none ($FF), clears IY-$2B bit 4.
  4. Enters the handler with HL = the parameters, A = $FF and NZ.
  .2C17 The handler, under
  .2C18 the return to CMD_FINISH
  .2C1D An EXDOS command is running
  .2C21 C = the characters after the word
  .2C24 HL = the word's last character
  .2C29 One more, for SKIP_BLANKS's count

@3D94 SKIP_BLANKS_C
  Steps HL over spaces and tabs, C counting the characters left.
  Entry: HL = the text, C = the characters left + 1.
  Exit: NC, A = the character at HL, Z when it is 0.
  Error: C and Z when the text ends.
  Changes: AF, C, HL.
  Keeps: B, DE, IX, IY.
  .3D94 Text used up?
  .3D97 A space?
  .3D9C A tab? Others end the skip

@2C2A CMD_PARAM_SKIP
  Skips the blanks (and 0 bytes) in front of the parameters.
  .2C2E The end: no parameters
  .2C30 A 0 byte: skip it too

@2C32 CMD_PARAM_MOVE
  Writes the parameters' length over the string's count byte and copies them behind it.
  .2C32 C = the characters left
  .2C35 The parameters' start, for the handler
  .2C36 None to copy?
  .2C38 Move them to the front

@3D10 FBLK_SET_DRIVE
  Gives the file block at IX drive A: IX+$06 = IX+$07 = A, IX+$00 = A - 1.
  Entry: A = the drive (0 = the default), IX = the block.
  Exit: DE = IX; C = A on entry; A = A - 1.
  Changes: AF, C, DE.
  Keeps: B, HL, IX, IY.
  .3D16 IX+$07: the drive
  .3D1A A - 1 into the block's first byte

@2C3A CMD_PARAM_END
  Ends the parameters with a 0 and prepares the handler's state, then "returns" into it.
  .2C3B The 0 at the end
  .2C3C The output buffer at IY+$6E
  .2C3F Empty (A = 0)
  .2C40 IX = IY+$FD, a file block for the command
  .2C47 Drive 0 (the default); A = $FF
  .2C4A HL = the parameters
  .2C4B No channel of the command's yet ($FF)
  .2C55 NZ
  .2C56 Into the handler; it returns to CMD_FINISH

@2C57 CMD_JUMP
  Jumps to the handler of a bit-7 name, without CMD_RUN's preparations.

@326F CLOSE_CHAN_IY
  Closes the channel whose number is in the byte at IY - $100 + E - 1 ($FF: none), then marks
  it $FF.
  Entry: E = the low byte of the offset (IY-$100+E points just above the byte), IY.
  Exit: HL = the byte; Z, A = 0 when there was no channel, else A = EXOS's status.
  Changes: AF, BC, DE, HL.
  Keeps: IX, IY.
  .3272 DE = $FF00 + E

@3275 CLOSE_CHAN_BELOW
  Closes the channel whose number is in the byte below HL ($FF: none) and marks it $FF.
  Entry: HL = one above the byte.
  Exit: HL = the byte; Z, A = 0 when there was no channel, else A = EXOS's status.
  Changes: AF, BC, DE, HL.
  Keeps: IX, IY.
  .3276 The channel number
  .3277 $FF: none
  .327A Close it (EXOS 3)
  .327C None now

@2D41 CMD_FINISH
  Where an EXDOS command returns (CMD_RUN pushed it):
  1. Closes the command's channels at IY-$2C and IY-$2D.
  2. Has every drive's changed buffers written back and dropped (function 14, B = C = $FF).
  3. Error $E5 (STOP) puts $20 back in FLAG_SOFT_IRQ ($BFF2) for the caller to see.
  4. Goes on at CMD_READY.
  Entry: A and the flags = the command's result.
  Exit: A, B, DE kept.
  .2D41 The command's result
  .2D44 IY-$2C, then IY-$2D
  .2D4C B = C = $FF: all drives, buffers dropped
  .2D4F EXDOS function 14
  .2D57 Error $E5 (STOP)?
  .2D5B FLAG_SOFT_IRQ = $20: the STOP key again

@2D60 CMD_READY
  Accepts EXDOS commands again (IY-$2B bit 0) and puts back the byte at IY-$58 (the write mode
  of $0AB0) that $3570 parked in IY-$29: $FE gives $FF, $FF gives 0; 0 there means none.
  .2D60 EXDOS commands accepted again
  .2D64 A parked value?
  .2D6A $FE/$FF become $FF/0
  .2D70 None parked now

@2D9A CMD_NOTHING
  The handler of "**" and "REM": does nothing.
  Entry: none.
  Exit: A = 0, Z, NC.
  Changes: AF.
  Keeps: BC, DE, HL, IX, IY.

@2D9C CMD_EXDOS_INFO
  The handler of the hidden 6-character name "EXDOS" + $FD (bit 7 set: run at once): hands
  back the workspace's address and B = $13.
  Entry: A = the handler address's low byte ($9C), as CMD_FOUND leaves it.
  Exit: DE = IY (the workspace + $80); B = $13; C = A.
  Changes: BC, DE.
  Keeps: AF, HL, IX, IY.
  .2D9F B = $13 (1.3, the version the help text gives?)

@3DFA COUNT_WORD
  Makes the word at HL a counted name: writes its length (up to a 0, space, tab or "/") into
  the byte before it.
  Entry: HL = the word.
  Exit: DE = the count byte; HL = the word's end; A = C = the length.
  Changes: AF, C, DE, HL.
  Keeps: B, IX, IY.
  .3DFA The count goes here
  .3DFC Counts from 0

@3DDB OPEN_PARAM_CHAN
  The channel a parameter names: "#n" is channel n, already open; any other word is a file
  name, opened on a free channel (OPEN_FREE_CHAN).
  Entry: HL = the parameter.
  Exit: NC, Z: E = channel n, D = 0, A = 0, HL past it (and a ":"); C: E = the channel
  opened, HL = past the name.
  Error: NZ, A = the open's status.
  Changes: AF, BC, DE, HL.
  Keeps: IX, IY.
  .3DDB "#"?
  .3DE0 DE = the name, counted
  .3DE4 Open it on a free channel
  .3DE7 E = the channel
  .3DE9 Failed?
  .3DEA C: a channel EXDOS opened

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The EXDOS command: the command interpreter

@2DA3 CMD_EXDOS_SHELL
  The handler of "EXDOS": runs EXDOS's command interpreter.
  1. Closes the input channel an earlier run opened (IY-$31); the input is the editor's
     channel (IY-$2F) again (IY-$2E), IY-$2B bit 3 clear.
  2. A parameter names the input instead (OPEN_PARAM_CHAN, IY-$37 bit 4 set meanwhile): "#n"
     or a file of commands; IY-$2B bit 3 then marks input not from the editor. Nothing may
     follow it ($9F, "Wrong number of parameters").
  3. When the interpreter is already running (IY-$2F not $FF) that is all; otherwise it
     starts (SHELL_START).
  Entry: HL = the parameters (zero-ended), IY.
  Exit: A = 0 when only the input changed; else as the interpreter ends (SHELL_CLOSE).
  Error: NZ, A = the error code.
  Changes: AF, BC, DE, HL, IX, all alternates.
  Keeps: IY.
  .2DA4 Close the channel at IY-$31
  .2DA9 The editor's channel
  .2DAC is the input
  .2DAF The input is the editor's
  .2DB4 A parameter?
  .2DBC Its channel: E
  .2DC7 A channel of ours (C)?
  .2DC9 Then the command's end closes it if anything fails

@3C14 REST_BLANK_END
  Checks that nothing but spaces and tabs is left before the text's 0.
  Entry: DE = the text.
  Exit: Z, NC, A = 0, DE past the 0.
  Error: A = $9F ("Wrong number of parameters"), NZ.
  Changes: AF, C, DE.
  Keeps: B, HL, IX, IY.
  .3C14 The next character

@2DCC SHELL_INPUT_SET
  The parameter's channel becomes the input (IY-$2E); one EXDOS opened is kept in IY-$31
  until the next run, no longer closed by CMD_FINISH.
  .2DCC Open failed?
  .2DCD Carry: a channel of ours
  .2DCE HL = the channel, DE = the text
  .2DCF Nothing after it?
  .2DD3 The input is not the editor
  .2DD7 The input channel
  .2DDB An open channel ("#n")?
  .2DDD Ours: closed by the next EXDOS
  .2DE0 not by CMD_FINISH

@2F17 SHELL_VARS_PTR
  DE = the save area for EXOS variables (IY+$F0), HL = the table of variables and their new
  values at $2FB7 (a variable number and a value each, 0 at the end).
  Entry: IY.
  Exit: DE = IY+$F0; HL = $2FB7.
  Changes: F, DE, HL.
  Keeps: A, BC, IX, IY.

@2DE4 SHELL_START
  Starts the interpreter unless it already runs (the editor's channel at IY-$2F is open).
  .2DE5 The editor's channel
  .2DE8 Open ($FF: none)? Done, A = 0
  .2DEA DE = the save area, HL = the table

@2DED SHELL_SET_VARS
  Sets up the interpreter's channels:
  1. Each EXOS variable of the table at $2FB7 is saved at IY+$F0 and set: 4 to 0, 22-25 (the
     video page: MODE_VID 0, 23 0, 24 40, 25 20), VID_EDIT 0, 30 0, 31 4, FLG_EDIT $1C,
     $4E and $4D $FF, 11 $1E, 10 3. IY-$2B bit 7 records that they need putting back.
  2. SHELL_CLOSE ($2F45) becomes the return address.
  3. The keyboard channel is the first that answers @@JOY (FIND_KBD_CHAN), else "KEYBOARD:"
     opened (IY-$2B bit 2: close it at the end).
  .2DED C = the variable
  .2DF0 Read it (B = 0)
  .2DF3 D = its value
  .2DF5 into the save area
  .2DF8 C = the variable again
  .2DFA D = its new value
  .2DFC Write it (B = 1)
  .2E01 The table's end?
  .2E04 The variables need putting back
  .2E08 SHELL_CLOSE: where the interpreter returns
  .2E0C A keyboard channel?
  .2E11 "KEYBOARD:"
  .2E14 Open it on a free channel
  .2E17 Failed
  .2E18 Ours: close it at the end

@2E1C SHELL_OPEN_CHANS
  Gives the editor its keyboard (EXOS variable 30), opens "VIDEO:" (IY-$30; variable 29,
  VID_EDIT) and writes the 10 bytes at $2F3E to it, opens "EDITOR:" (IY-$2F; variable 4),
  which is the input unless a parameter named one.
  .2E1C IY-$32: the keyboard channel
  .2E20 Variable 30 = the keyboard channel
  .2E25 "VIDEO:"
  .2E2B Failed
  .2E2C IY-$30: the video channel
  .2E30 The 10 bytes at $2F3E (ESC "C" ...)
  .2E39 VID_EDIT = the video channel
  .2E3E "EDITOR:"
  .2E44 Failed
  .2E45 IY-$2F: the editor's channel
  .2E49 Variable 4 = the editor's channel
  .2E4E Input named by a parameter?
  .2E54 No: the editor is the input

@2E57 SHELL_BANNER
  Input from the editor: a space and the version line ("EXDOS version 1.3") on channel $FF.
  .2E57 A space

@2E63 SHELL_LOOP
  One command line, until IY-$2B bit 1 asks to end:
  1. The status line (from the word at $BFF6, + 6) shows " EXDOS" (bit 7 set on the letters,
     the 6 bytes at $2F38) and message $3E ("command interpreter").
  2. From the editor: a ":" prompt, and the video page shown (EXOS 11, @@DISP).
  .2E5F EXDOS commands accepted; the loop runs on
  .2E67 HL = the status line's text
  .2E6E " EXDOS", bit 7 set on the letters
  .2E77 Message $3E: "  command interpreter      "
  .2E7C Past its count byte
  .2E81 Input from the editor?
  .2E85 Then a ":" prompt
  .2E8A The video channel
  .2E8D B = 1 (@@DISP): its first row at screen row 1
  .2E90 20 rows
  .2E95 Failed: end with the error

@2FF0 READ_LINE
  Reads a line from channel C (EXOS 5) into the output buffer at IY+$6E: a count byte, the
  characters (codes from $20; at most 128) and a 0. The line ends at CR or ESC; other codes
  below $20, and characters past the 128th, are dropped. Code $1A (end of text) is error $E4.
  Entry: C = the channel, IY.
  Exit: Z, A = 0; B = the code that ended the line; HL = the buffer.
  Error: NZ, NC, A = EXOS's status, or $E4 for code $1A.
  Changes: AF, B, DE, HL.
  Keeps: C, IX, IY.
  .2FF3 DE = the count byte
  .2FF5 Count 0

@2E96 SHELL_READ
  Reads the command line (READ_LINE) from the input channel, FLG_EDIT set to $1C first. A line
  of 128 characters or more is error $9C ("Command too long").
  .2E99 FLG_EDIT = $1C
  .2E9D The input channel
  .2EA0 Read the line
  .2EA3 Failed?
  .2EA5 The count: 128 or more?
  .2EA8 Then error $9C: Command too long

@2F23 STOP_CHECK
  Takes the STOP key into a status: A = 0 becomes $E5 when FLAG_SOFT_IRQ ($BFF2) holds $20;
  $E5 (from a key or given) clears FLAG_SOFT_IRQ.
  Entry: A = a status, with flags from OR A (Z for 0).
  Exit: Z, A = 0 when there is no error and no STOP.
  Error: NZ, A = the status, or $E5 for STOP.
  Changes: AF.
  Keeps: BC, DE, HL, IX, IY.
  .2F23 No error: look at the STOP key
  .2F25 STOP ($E5)?
  .2F27 Another error: kept

@2F28 STOP_CLEAR
  Clears FLAG_SOFT_IRQ and returns error $E5 (NZ).
  .2F2C NZ, NC

@2F2F STOP_FLAG_TEST
  FLAG_SOFT_IRQ = $20 (the STOP key)?
  .2F36 No: A = 0, Z

@2FD2 CRLF_TO_DEFCH
  Writes CR and LF to channel $FF.
  Entry: none.
  Exit: AF, C as on entry.
  Changes: B, DE.
  Keeps: AF, C, HL, IX, IY.
  .2FD2 CR
  .2FD7 LF

@2EAA SHELL_LINE_STAT
  The line's status, STOP included: on error, $E4 (the input ended) ends the interpreter;
  another error goes on a new line and is reported.
  .2EAA STOP pressed?
  .2EAF Input ended ($E4)?
  .2EB3 A new line before the report

@2EB8 SHELL_INPUT_END
  The input has ended: the interpreter ends after this line.

@2EBC SHELL_LINE_OK
  A line from the editor ended by ESC leaves the interpreter when the keyboard channel's status
  (EXOS 9) gives C = 0; otherwise the line is taken as a command line.
  .2EBC Input from a file or channel?
  .2EC3 Ended by ESC?
  .2EC7 The keyboard channel's status
  .2ECE C = 0?
  .2ECF No: read the line again
  .2ED1 End the interpreter
  .2ED5 A = 0

@2ED7 SHELL_EXEC
  Runs the line as a command string.
  .2ED7 C = the line's length
  .2ED8 + 1, for SKIP_BLANKS

@2ED9 SHELL_SKIP_LEAD
  Skips the leading spaces, tabs and colons; an empty line is not run.
  .2ED9 The next character
  .2EDA Skip blanks
  .2EDD An empty line
  .2EDF ":"? Skip it too
  .2EE3 The count goes in front of the first character
  .2EE5 DE = the command string
  .2EE6 To every extension (EXOS 26)
  .2EE8 STOP pressed meanwhile?
  .2EEB Done
  .2EED Error $A4 (Disk operation aborted)?
  .2EF1 Or STOP ($E5)?

@2EF5 SHELL_ERROR
  An error from a file of commands, $A4 or STOP ends the interpreter with it.
  .2EF5 Input from a file or channel?

@2EFA SHELL_ERR_SHOW
  Reports the error: "***" (message $3C) on channel $FF, then the error's text through the
  output buffer.
  .2EFB Message $3C: "\***  "
  .2F01 HL = IY-$23, for the code's digits
  .2F08 The error's text into the buffer
  .2F0B A new line: the buffer goes out

@2F0E SHELL_NEXT
  Back for the next line unless IY-$2B bit 1 asks to end.
  .2F0E The end?
  .2F15 A = 0

@2F44 SHELL_END_STOP
  STOP during the pause: its status $E5 replaces the saved one.
  .2F44 Drop the saved status

@2F45 SHELL_CLOSE
  Where the interpreter returns (SHELL_SET_VARS pushed it). After a file of commands that ran
  to its end, a pause of 65536 STOP checks; then SHELL_RESTORE.
  Entry: A, F = the interpreter's status.
  .2F45 Keep the interpreter's status
  .2F46 An error?
  .2F48 Input from the editor?
  .2F4E The pause: 65536 STOP checks

@2F50 SHELL_PAUSE_ROW
  B = 0: 256 checks for each count of C.

@2F51 SHELL_PAUSE
  One STOP check of the pause (after a file of commands).
  .2F52 STOP pressed?
  .2F55 Yes: end at once with $E5

@2F5C SHELL_RESTORE
  Ends the interpreter:
  1. Blanks the status line's 32 characters.
  2. Closes the editor's, the video and the input file's channels (IY-$2F, IY-$30, IY-$31),
     and the keyboard's when EXDOS opened it (IY-$2B bit 2).
  3. IY-$2B bit 1 set (the end), bit 2 clear; the EXOS variables saved at IY+$F0 get their
     values back.
  Exit: A, F = the interpreter's status.
  .2F5C HL = the status line's text
  .2F63 32 spaces

@2F66 STATUS_BLANK
  Writes B spaces from HL.
  .2F6A IY-$2F: the editor's channel
  .2F6F IY-$30: the video channel
  .2F72 IY-$31: the input file's channel
  .2F75 The keyboard's, if EXDOS opened it
  .2F7C Not to close again
  .2F80 The interpreter has ended
  .2F84 Hides PUSH AF at $2F85, the way in from $2D8D
  .2F86 The variables are put back now
  .2F8A DE = the saved values, HL = the table

@2F8D VARS_RESTORE
  Puts each EXOS variable of the table back to its saved value; then A and the flags come
  back from the stack.
  .2F8D C = the variable
  .2F8E HL = the next entry
  .2F90 Its saved value
  .2F93 D = the value
  .2F94 Write it (B = 1)
  .2F99 The table's end?
  .2F9C The status

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Line input and output helpers

@2FE2 READ_LINE_IN
  Sends the output buffer out, sets FLG_EDIT to $1C and reads a line (READ_LINE) from the
  channel at IY-$51.
  Entry: IY.
  Exit: Z, A = 0; B = the code that ended the line; HL = the output buffer.
  Error: NZ, A = the status ($E4 for code $1A).
  Changes: AF, BC, DE, HL.
  Keeps: IX, IY.
  .2FE2 Send the buffer out
  .2FE6 FLG_EDIT = $1C
  .2FED C = the channel

@2FF6 READ_LINE_PUT
  Stores the character at DE and a 0 after it.
  .2FF6 The character (0 at the start)
  .2FF8 The 0 after it

@2FFA READ_LINE_CHAR
  Reads the next character (EXOS 5).
  .2FFC A = the channel
  .2FFF Failed?
  .3001 B = the character
  .3002 Code $1A?
  .3004 Carry: a character
  .3007 Error $E4, NC

@300A READ_LINE_GOT
  An error returns (NC); CR and ESC end the line; codes below $20 are dropped, as is any
  character once 128 are in.
  .300C An error
  .300D B = the character
  .300E CR?
  .3012 ESC?
  .3016 A control code: drop it
  .301A 128 in?
  .301E One more

@3021 READ_LINE_END
  The line is complete: A = 0, Z.

@305A OUTBUF_CHAR_END
  OUTBUF_CHAR's end: a character from $1B up returns; a control code first sends the buffer
  out (OUTBUF_FLUSH).
  .305D NC: a character from $1B up

@3077 OUTBUF_TEXT_NEXT
  Steps DE to the text's next character (OUTBUF_TEXT's loop).

@3080 OUTBUF_FBLK_DRV
  Writes the drive letter and ":" of the file block at IX (its IX+$07, OUTBUF_DRIVE).
  Entry: IX = the file block, IY.
  Exit: A = 0 and Z, or EXOS's status.
  Changes: AF.
  Keeps: BC, DE, HL, IX, IY.

@309E OUTBUF_CHARS
  Writes B characters from HL to the output buffer.
  Entry: B = the count, HL = the characters, IY.
  Exit: HL past them; B = 0; A = 0 and Z, or the last write's status.
  Changes: AF, B, HL.
  Keeps: C, DE, IX, IY.

@30CF OUTBUF_SPACE
  Writes a space to the output buffer.
  Entry: IY.
  Exit: A = 0 and Z, or EXOS's status.
  Changes: AF.
  Keeps: BC, DE, HL, IX, IY.

@308C OUTBUF_NAME
  Writes a directory entry's 8-character name and 3-character extension (from BC + 8):
  - Z: as they are, a space between ("NAME     EXT").
  - NZ and C: the name without its spaces, "." (a space when there is no extension), the
    extension, a space.
  - NZ and NC: the same with the name's spaces in front of it (right-aligned in 8).
  Entry: BC + 8 = the name; F as above; IY.
  Exit: A = 0 and Z, or EXOS's status.
  Changes: AF, BC, HL.
  Keeps: DE, IX, IY.
  .308D HL = the name
  .3092 Short form?
  .3094 The 8 name characters
  .3099 A space

@309C OUTBUF_CHARS3
  Writes 3 characters from HL (the extension).
  Entry: HL = the characters, IY.
  Exit: HL past them; A = 0 and Z, or the last write's status.
  Changes: AF, B, HL.
  Keeps: C, DE, IX, IY.

@30A6 NAME_SHORT
  The short form; carry: the name without padding.
  .30A6 8 characters
  .30A9 Not right-aligned?
  .30AB HL = past the name; B = 8

@30AD NAME_PAD
  A space for each space in the name: they go in front.
  .30AF A space?
  .30B1 Then one in front

@30B6 NAME_PLAIN
  B = 8: the name's characters without its spaces.

@30B7 NAME_PLAIN_LOOP
  Writes the name's characters except spaces, then picks the extension's separator.
  .30B9 Spaces are left out
  .30C0 The extension's first character
  .30C1 None (a space)?
  .30C5 Else "."

@30C7 NAME_EXT
  Writes the separator (a space or "."), the extension and a space.
  .30CA The 3 extension characters

@30CD OUTBUF_ATTR
  Writes character A for NZ (an attribute that is set), else a space.
  Entry: A = the character; F: NZ to write it; IY.
  Exit: A = 0 and Z, or EXOS's status.
  Changes: AF.
  Keeps: BC, DE, HL, IX, IY.

@30D3 OUTBUF_SIZE
  Writes the 4-byte number at HL: below 1024 as "<n> byte(s)" (OUTBUF_COUNTWORD); from 1024 as
  "<n>K", n = the number / 1024 (the number at HL is shifted in place).
  Entry: HL = the number (low byte first), IY.
  Exit: A = 0 and Z, or EXOS's status.
  Changes: AF, BC, DE, HL, DE', L'.
  Keeps: IX, IY.
  .30D4 DE = the low word
  .30DA The high word not 0?
  .30DD 1024 or more?
  .30E2 HL = the number; DE = its address
  .30E4 Message $36: " byte"

@31E6 OUTBUF_DEC16
  Writes HL in decimal (no padding) to the output buffer: it goes into IY-$26 as a 4-byte
  number and $31FD writes that.
  Entry: HL = the number, IY.
  Exit: Z.
  Changes: AF, BC, DE, HL, DE', L'.
  Keeps: IX, IY.
  .31E9 BC = the output buffer
  .31EF HL = IY-$23, the number's top byte
  .31F8 then HL

@30E6 OUTBUF_COUNTWORD
  Writes a count and a word: HL in decimal, message A (" byte" or " file"), then the plural
  unless the count is 1: "s" in English; in German "s" for " byte" and "en" for another
  word (EXOS variable $90 = 0 chooses German).
  Entry: A = the word's message number, HL = the count, IY.
  Exit: A = 0 and Z, or EXOS's status; Z with A = the message number for a count of 1.
  Changes: AF, BC, DE, HL, DE', L'.
  Keeps: IX, IY.
  .30E9 The count in decimal
  .30ED The word
  .30F1 A count of 1?
  .30F5 A = the message number
  .30F6 Then singular
  .30F9 Read EXOS variable $90
  .3100 0: German
  .3103 A = the message number
  .3105 English: "s"
  .3107 German " byte": "s"
  .310B Otherwise "en"

@3114 PLURAL_S
  Writes "s".

@3118 SIZE_IN_K
  The number at HL (pointing at its top byte) is shifted right 10 times (divided by 1024),
  then written in decimal and "K".
  .3118 10 shifts

question 2D9C Which program sends the hidden command "EXDOS" + $FD, and does it read B = $13 as EXDOS's version and DE as its workspace?
question 2E30 What do the 10 bytes at $2F3E (ESC "C" ...) do on the VIDEO: channel, the last four being the code at $2F44?
question 2EC7 Does EXOS 9 on the keyboard channel give C = 0 when no key waits, so that ESC alone ends the interpreter?
feedback wrong 3DDB Keeps: none (IY may change) only because $0336's return was not followed; OPEN_FREE_CHAN keeps IX and IY, so $3DDB and $2DA3 keep IY
feedback missing 2B5E-2BF1 The cards say only "jump $0037"/"jump $0033"; that these are extension actions 3 and 2 of the dispatcher at $0013 (B, DE, C as EXOS hands them) had to come from the listing
feedback pattern 2B69-2B98 $2B69 and $2B6E end with EXX: every call swaps the register sets, so HELP_EXDOS's list runs in the other set; the register facts cannot show the swap count
feedback missing 2E30 The write at $2E30 reads 10 bytes from $2F3E, running past the bytes region $2F38-$2F43 into the code at $2F44-$2F47; no card notes a data read that overlaps code
feedback listing 29E4-2B5D The range starts inside $29E4 and ends inside $2B2E: the stack layout the fragments share (three words, then four) had to be worked out from the listing across ranges 8 and 9
